  "live_records": {
    "q-184672": {
      "dom_id": "step-q184672",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Suppose we wish to construct a proof by contrapositive of the following statement:\n\nLet $n$ be an integer. Then $n$ is odd whenever ${n}^{3}-1$ is even.\n\nA proof schema is outlined below. Fill in the missing entries.\n\nThe contrapositive form of the statement is as follows:\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\,⇒\\,{{field-2}}\n$$\n\nIf $n$ is {{field-3}}, then $n=$ {{field-4}}, where $a$ is {{field-5}}.\n\nSubstituting {{field-6}} into the expression ${n}^{3}-1,$ we can show that ${n}^{3}-1=$ {{field-7}}, where $b$ is {{field-8}}.\n\nTherefore, since ${n}^{3}-1$ {{field-9}} a multiple of $2,$ we conclude that ${n}^{3}-1$ is {{field-10}}.",
      "worked_solution": "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p.$\n\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1.$\n\nIn this case, we need to prove the following:\n\n$$\n{n}^{3}-1\\text{ is even}\\,⇒\\,n\\text{ is odd}\n$$\n\nTo prove $P⇒Q,$ it suffices to prove the contrapositive $¬Q⇒¬P.$\n\nThe contrapositive form of the given statement is\n\n$$\nn\\text{ is not odd}\\,⇒\\,{n}^{3}-1\\text{ is not even}\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\nn\\text{ is even}\\,⇒\\,{n}^{3}-1\\text{ is odd}.\n$$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is even.\n\nIf $n$ is $\\text{even},$ then\n$n=2a,$\nwhere $a$ is $\\text{an integer}.$\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a$ into the expression ${n}^{3}-1$ and show it is odd by writing it as  one more than a multiple of $2.$\n\nSubstituting $n=2a$ into the expression ${n}^{3}-1,$ we can show that ${n}^{3}-1=2b+1,$ where $b$ is $\\text{an integer}.$\n\nOnce we've done this, the proof is complete, and we can state our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, since ${n}^{3}-1$ $\\text{is one more than}$ a multiple of $2,$ we conclude that ${n}^{3}-1$ is $\\text{odd}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
