    "q-184783": {
      "dom_id": "step-q184783",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Let $n$ be an integer. Using the contrapositive, prove that $n$ is odd provided that $7n+6$ is odd.\n\nThe contrapositive form of the given statement is as follows:\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\,⇒\\,{{field-2}}\n$$\n\nWe'll prove this statement instead of the original one.\n\nIf $n$ is {{field-3}}, then $n=$ {{field-4}} for some integer $a.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7n+6 & ={{field-5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $b=$ {{field-6}}. Since $a$ is {{field-7}}, we have that $b$ is {{field-8}}.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7n+6 & ={{field-9}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich {{field-10}} a multiple of $2.$ Hence, $7n+6$ is {{field-11}}.",
      "worked_solution": "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p.$\n\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1.$\n\nIn this case, we need to prove the following:\n\n$$\n7n+6\\text{ is odd}\\,⇒\\,n\\text{ is odd}\n$$\n\nTo prove $P⇒Q,$ it suffices to prove the contrapositive $¬Q⇒¬P.$ So, let's write down the contrapositive of our statement.\n\nThe contrapositive form of the given statement is as follows:\n\n$$\nn\\text{ is even}\\,⇒\\,7n+6\\text{ is even}\n$$\n\nWe'll prove this statement instead of the original one.\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is even.\n\nIf $n$ is even, then $n=2a$ for some integer $a.$\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a$ into the expression $7n+6$ and show it is even by writing it as a multiple of  $2$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7n+6 & =7(2a)+6 \\\\  & =14a+6 \\\\  & =2{{(7a+3)}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we define another integer $b=7a+3$ (highlighted above), we have that $7n+6=2b,$ which shows that $7n+6$ is indeed  a multiple of $2.$\n\nLet $b=7a+3.$ Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7n+6 & =2{{(7a+3)}_{⏟}}_{b} \\\\  & =2b,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a multiple of $2.$\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, $7n+6$ is even. \n\nPROOF\n\nThe contrapositive form of the given statement is as follows:\n\n$$\nn\\text{ is even}\\,⇒\\,7n+6\\text{ is even}\n$$\n\nWe'll prove this statement instead of the original one.\n\nIf $n$ is even, then $n=$ $2a$ for some integer $a.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7n+6 & =2(7a+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $b=$ $7a+3$. Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7n+6 & =2b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a multiple of $2.$ Hence, $7n+6$ is even.",
